## 4. `platform-starrocks` — connection, query engine, base repository

### 4.1 Configuration

```yaml
# Each service's application.yml
spring:
  application:
    name: service-orders
  threads:
    virtual:
      enabled: true          # Boot 3.2+, Java 21
  jackson:
    serialization:
      write-dates-as-timestamps: false

starrocks:
  # Connector/J balances across multiple FE nodes; StarRocks speaks MySQL protocol on 9030
  jdbc-url: >-
    jdbc:mysql:loadbalance://fe-1:9030,fe-2:9030,fe-3:9030/dw
    ?useSSL=false
    &characterEncoding=UTF-8
    &connectTimeout=3000
    &socketTimeout=65000
    &useServerPrepStmts=false
    &cachePrepStmts=false
    &rewriteBatchedStatements=false
    &tcpKeepAlive=true
    &loadBalanceBlocklistTimeout=30000
    &loadBalanceStrategy=random
  username: ${STARROCKS_USER}
  password: ${STARROCKS_PASSWORD}
  query-timeout-seconds: 30
  slow-query-threshold-ms: 2000
  pool:
    maximum-pool-size: 32      # OLAP: few, long queries. Don't go huge; StarRocks FE has a connection cap.
    minimum-idle: 4
    connection-timeout-ms: 3000
    max-lifetime-ms: 1800000
    keepalive-time-ms: 300000

management:
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health,info,metrics,prometheus
  endpoint:
    health:
      show-details: when_authorized
```

> `useServerPrepStmts=false` is deliberate: StarRocks' server-side prepared statement support is limited; client-side substitution is fully supported and still parameter-safe.

